WEBVTT

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.450
By the end of this course, you've learned
neurodiversity describes the idea

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:10.267
that people experience
and interact with the

00:00:10.291 --> 00:00:12.250
world around them in
many different ways.

00:00:12.829 --> 00:00:15.589
There is no one right
way of thinking, learning,

00:00:15.628 --> 00:00:19.140
and behaving, and differences
should not be viewed as deficits.

00:00:19.881 --> 00:00:23.524
You learned that the neurodiverse
population remains a largely untapped

00:00:23.649 --> 00:00:27.394
pool of talent, and they have the
ability to bring in new perspectives.

00:00:28.316 --> 00:00:32.120
You learned that neurodivergent employees
are proven to have better pattern

00:00:32.121 --> 00:00:36.457
recognition and creative thinking than
their neurotypical counterparts.

00:00:36.917 --> 00:00:39.468
Plus, accommodating
neurodivergent candidates

00:00:39.492 --> 00:00:41.808
can greatly improve
a company's image.

00:00:42.222 --> 00:00:46.240
You learned that people managers must
practice inclusive recruiting with,

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.561
for example, psychometric
assessments or performance-based

00:00:49.562 --> 00:00:52.660
tests, so as not to miss
out on neurodivergent talent.

00:00:53.647 --> 00:00:58.260
You learned that leaders must accommodate
neurodivergent employees by creating

00:00:58.261 --> 00:01:02.906
distraction-free workspaces, providing
flexibility, and educating themselves

00:01:02.931 --> 00:01:06.945
and their team about the challenges and
uniqueness of people on the spectrum.